In Day 1, you learned how Deployments ensure Pod count and how Services discover Pod IPs using EndpointSlices. Today, we dive into two critical production concepts:

Application-Level Health Checking: Preventing broken Pods from receiving traffic (Readiness) or restarting deadlock processes (Liveness).

Stateful Storage: Storing data so it survives Pod deletions and restarts using Persistent Volumes (PV) and Persistent Volume Claims (PVC).

Part 1: Health Probes (Liveness vs. Readiness vs. Startup)

By default, Kubernetes only checks if the container's main process is running. If your app deadlocks,
hangs on an infinite loop, or loses its DB connection while keeping the process alive, Kubernetes thinks
it is healthy. Probes solve this.

Container Health Probes
Kubernetes natively detects crashed processes (PID 1 exits), but it cannot detect application-level deadlocks, frozen event loops, or slow warmups without active probes.


|   Probe Type   |                        What it monitors                       |                                           Action when it fails                                          |
|:--------------:|:-------------------------------------------------------------:|:-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------:|
| startupProbe   | Is the legacy/heavy application finished booting up?          | Disables liveness/readiness checks until it succeeds. Kills and restarts the container if it times out. |
| readinessProbe | Is the application ready to accept user traffic?              | Removes the Pod IP from the Service/EndpointSlice. The container is not restarted.                      |
| livenessProbe  | Is the application in an unrecoverable deadlock/frozen state? | Restarts the container (kubelet kills and recreates the container process)                              |


| Probe Type     | The Question It Asks                                        | Action on Failure                                                                                           | Typical DevOps Use Case                                                                            |
|----------------|-------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| Probe          | "Has the container finished booting up?"                    | Kills and restarts the container; temporarily disables liveness and readiness checks.                       | Heavy JVM/Spring apps, database schema migrations, warming large internal memory caches.           |
| readinessProbe | "Is the container ready to process live traffic?"           | Detaches the Pod IP from the EndpointSlice / Service. The container keeps running; no restart is triggered. | Gracefully draining traffic during transient backend slowness or downstream dependency reconnects. |
| livenessProbe  | "Is the process stuck in an unrecoverable freeze/deadlock?" | Restarts the container (kubelet terminates PID 1 and restarts the container according to restartPolicy).    | Catching fatal execution freezes, locked threads, or unhandled exceptions.                         |

